Don't panic when crosvm's own SIGKILL to its process group doesn't reach it.

cleanup() kills the process group when a jailed device process hasn't exited
within its wait, and kill_process_group() then asserts the caller is dead.
Run as the init of its own pid namespace (systemd PrivatePIDs=, as the sandbox
VMs are), crosvm is immune to signals it sends itself: kill() returns, the
unreachable!() aborts, and every such stop ends in a core dump and a failed
unit. The rest of the group is dead by then; return, and let crosvm exit
(which, as the namespace's init, takes anything left in it).

--- a/base/src/sys/linux/mod.rs
+++ b/base/src/sys/linux/mod.rs
@@ -388,6 +388,8 @@
 pub fn kill_process_group() -> Result<()> {
     // SAFETY: Safe because pid is 'self group' and return value doesn't matter.
     unsafe { kill(0, SIGKILL) }?;
-    // Kill succeeded, so this process never reaches here.
-    unreachable!();
+    // Kill succeeded, so this process only gets here as the init of its pid
+    // namespace, which its own signals don't reach; the rest of the group is
+    // dead, and its exit takes the namespace.
+    Ok(())
 }
